.\" Manpage for dirhash.
.TH DIRHASH 1 "May 2026" "dir-hash 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dirhash \- content-based directory tree hash
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dirhash
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-e ]
.IR path \ ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dirhash
computes a BLAKE3 content fingerprint for each
.I path
argument. A path may be a regular file or a directory.
.PP
For a directory,
.B dirhash
walks the tree, hashes every regular file with BLAKE3, and folds the
sorted multiset of child hashes into a Merkle-style hash for each
subdirectory \(em bottom up. The result is name-agnostic: two
directory trees with identical content under different filenames hash
to the same value. Symlinks and other non-regular entries are excluded.
.PP
By default,
.B dirhash
prints one line per
.I path
argument in
.BR md5sum (1)-compatible
form, with directories distinguished by a trailing slash:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
<hex>  <path>
<hex>  <path>/
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
Recursive: print a hash line for every node visited (post-order \(em
children before their parent), not just the
.I path
roots.
.TP
.B \-t
Tagged output, in BSD
.BR md5 (1)
style:
.RS
.PP
.B "BLAKE3 (path) = <hex>"
.RE
.TP
.B \-e
Print per-file I/O errors to standard error. Without this flag,
unreadable files are silently skipped.
.TP
.B \-h
Show usage and exit.
.SH "I/O BEHAVIOUR"
Every file is opened with
.B POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL
and closed with
.BR POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED ,
which avoids polluting the page cache. Files are read in 4 MiB chunks.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B dirhash
exits 0 on success and non-zero if at least one
.I path
argument could not be hashed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Hash a single backup snapshot:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ dirhash /backup/2024-01
164ca99b...  /backup/2024-01/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compare two snapshots node-by-node:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ dirhash -r /backup/2024-01 | sort > a.list
$ dirhash -r /backup/2024-02 | sort > b.list
$ diff a.list b.list
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR dir_hash (3),
.BR md5sum (1),
.BR fts (3),
.BR posix_fadvise (2)
.SH AUTHORS
Keith Williams.
